{
  "topic": "What calling pandemic healthcare workers “heroes” commits us to when heroism is understood as achievement, sacrifice, or a public practice of assigning honour",
  "tension_system": {
    "primary": {
      "axis": "the proper basis of heroism",
      "poles": [
        "heroism requires exceptional, history-altering achievement",
        "heroism consists in accepting grave sacrifice for others, even within an ordinary professional role"
      ],
      "fate": "displaced"
    },
    "secondary": {
      "axis": "the public function of heroic praise",
      "poles": [
        "recognition of individual courage",
        "concealment of institutional obligations and preventable exposure"
      ],
      "fate": "left_open"
    },
    "interaction": "Once heroism is treated as an act of public classification rather than a discoverable personal property, the achievement-sacrifice dispute becomes inseparable from what praise permits institutions to demand."
  },
  "paragraph_briefs": [
    {
      "para": 1,
      "gist": "Open with the abstract claim that moral titles appear to describe conduct but also rank it. Stage heroic exceptionalists, for whom Omaha Beach and Carlyle’s world-shaping figures set the standard, against sacrificial egalitarians, for whom COVID-19 clinicians merit the title by knowingly bearing danger for strangers. Initially suggest that the dispute concerns how much risk or consequence is enough, then begin relocating it: the camps are using “hero” to perform different acts—historical elevation and moral gratitude—so comparing degrees of danger cannot settle the question."
    },
    {
      "para": 2,
      "gist": "Raise the stakes by showing that pandemic hero-language reached beyond private admiration into hospital signs, commercial toys and civic rituals. The exceptionalist hears a claim about rare stature and objects to inflation; the egalitarian hears overdue recognition of exposed labour and objects to exclusion. Their semantic divergence has practical force: defining assigned professional danger as heroic may honour workers, but may also recast shortages, unsafe conditions or institutional duties as occasions for individual nobility."
    },
    {
      "para": 3,
      "gist": "Press the case of a nurse screened at a hospital entrance before returning to hazardous but contracted work: neither world-historical consequence nor wholly voluntary self-sacrifice cleanly captures the conduct, yet withholding praise can seem morally obtuse. Set this against Carlyle’s description of heroes as “modellers, patterns, and in a wide sense creators” of what others attain. The contrast exposes rival definitions—hero as causal maker versus hero as exemplary bearer of burdens—and shows that choosing one reorganises whom society notices and what it owes them."
    },
    {
      "para": 4,
      "gist": "Conclude that the decisive object is not a scale capable of placing D-Day soldiers and pandemic clinicians at different heights. Even the passage’s definitions treat “hero” as though it were a neutral measuring instrument. The more consequential inquiry is how conferring the title distributes honour, excuses danger and frames duty—because heroism is partly made by the institutions and audiences that invoke it."
    }
  ],
